00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:47.340
11.35%. The current streak is 1 win, and our AI analysis continues learning from sector rotation

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:53.500
patterns. Imagine you bought shares of a newly public company on its first trading day at the offering

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:59.940
price. Your order filled cleanly, but the real supply pressure arrives 100-80 days later when

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:06.000
insiders can sell. The mechanism works through staggered release schedules where early investors and

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:12.000
employees face sequential lockups that create predictable volume spikes, often pushing the stock

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:19.660
8-15% lower in the week before each tranche expires. Most retail investors assume the post-IPO

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:25.940
pop is permanent and get caught holding through these engineered supply events. Professionals always

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:32.480
check the S1 filing for the exact lockup language, and set alerts 10 days before each scheduled release.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:40.040
The biggest mistake is treating the IPO price as a floor, instead always map the full lockup calendar

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:47.600
before sizing any position. Before we wrap, check XLF volume on Monday for any follow-through that

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:53.160
